# Build a character standing ADJACENT to an awake, hostile, perceiving
# monster, then SAVE him, so the pair can be frozen for tools/check_headless.sh
# steps 8-10 (bd inc-gjzx, docs/specs/2026-09-18-headless-prompt-fixtures-brief.md).
#
#   INCURSION_OPTIONS=tools/fixtures/options-2026-08-22.dat \
#       tools/headless.sh tools/keys/engaged-hostile-save.keys <seed>
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS. Steps 8-10 used to drive tools/keys/dive.keys and hope the
# generated world produced a death prompt or a threat prompt at a fixed key
# count. It no longer does (see the brief). This script instead puts a known
# character next to a known monster and saves that arrangement, so the check
# can load it and drive the two prompts on purpose.
#
# THE CHARGEN BELOW IS A COPY of tools/keys/lizardfolk-monk-save.keys,
# deliberately unchanged up to the point the character enters play. Do not
# "improve" it; a different character belongs in a new script and a new
# fixture, per tools/fixtures/README.md.
#
# THE MONSTER: a bugbear (lib/mon2.irh), summoned from the wizard menu and
# placed one square EAST of the player (RIGHT ENTER on the "Summon where?"
# targeting prompt).
#
#   - HOSTILE by construction, with no wizard forcing needed. Every monster's
#     default hostility to the player falls through to
#     TargetSystem::RacialHostility (src/Target.cpp:642-652): "evil creatures
#     are always looking for an advantage" makes any M_EVIL monster an Enemy
#     of the player outright, regardless of relative challenge rating, and
#     the bugbear carries M_EVIL (lib/mon2.irh:757). No ENEMY_TO stati, no
#     "All Monster Aggravate", nothing else is needed.
#   - AWAKE and PERCEIVING: Monster::Initialize is called with in_play=true
#     (src/Debug.cpp:685), which is exactly the branch that SKIPS the
#     HIDING setup in Monster::Initialize (src/Monster.cpp:1503, guarded by
#     "!in_play"), and nothing in the bugbear's own On Event handlers puts it
#     to sleep. Confirmed empirically, not just read: the loaded fixture asks
#     the threat prompt on the first move away (see threat-freeze.keys) and
#     it kills a resting character (see death-freeze.keys), both of which
#     src/Move.cpp:925-928 refuse for a hiding, sleeping, or otherwise
#     non-perceiving creature.
#   - WEAKEST-THAT-WORKS: goblin (Hit +0 vs the monk's Def 13, roughly a 40%
#     hit chance with a d6 weapon) was tried first and measured too slow --
#     see the fixture's own header for the actual rest-key count this script's
#     monster needed. Bugbear (Hit +5, Str 15, a morningstar for 1d8+2, Weapon
#     Focus) kills reliably well inside a two-hundred-key rest budget.
#
# THE SQUARE TO THE WEST of the player (where threat-freeze.keys steps) is
# ordinary room floor, the same open ground the character was created on;
# confirmed by running threat-freeze.keys and reading its dump, not assumed.
#
# Freeze Monsters is NOT touched: a frozen monster cannot land the killing
# blow death-freeze.keys and death-confirm.keys both need.

a       # Initial Choices: [a] Create a New Character
n       # Alter the character generation options? -- no
y       # Your options will place the game in Explore Mode. Continue? -- yes
h       # Race:     [h] Lizardfolk
a       # Subrace:  [a] Lizardfolk (Standard)
e       # Class:    [e] Monk
a       # Attributes: the first of the five offered sets
c       # Alignment: [c] Neutral
a       # First feat
a       # Second feat

# Skill Manager, twice. Three RIGHTs per skill spends everything available
# without needing to know how many ranks this character has.
RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN
RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN
ENTER y

y       # Do you wish to worship <god>? -- yes

RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN
RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN
ENTER y

# In the dungeon now. Three keys settle the opening: the room description
# wants dismissing, then two messages want acknowledging. A fourth ESC here
# opens the System Menu instead and every later key lands in it.
ESC ENTER SPACE

@dump:arrival

# ------------------------------------------------------------- the monster
w                              # invoke the wizard debug menu
y                               # "Enter wizard mode?" -- yes, first time only
@choose "Monster Summoning"
@choose "Goblinkin"
@choose "bugbear"
RIGHT ENTER                    # "Summon where?" -- one square east
@until "HP:" ESC

@dump:engaged

# ------------------------------------------------------------------- save
ESC                     # System Menu -- and NOT a cancel; see the warning in
                        # tools/keys/save-continue.keys
@expect "System Menu"   # refuse to press a letter into any other screen
b                       # [b] Save and Continue

@dump:saved

# NO @quit HERE, DELIBERATELY: it is Save and Quit (src/Wposix.cpp:1657-1664)
# and would write the save a second time, after the point this script names,
# and shuffle the first copy off to <name>.sav.backup. Running out of keys
# instead ends the session with exactly the one save above.
